Thought:[32;1m[1;3mQuestion: A consumer electronics factory operates an assembly line with three workstations and can produce 101 premium radio models: HiFi-1, HiFi-2, …, HiFi-101. The processing time required per unit of each model at each workstation is provided in workstation_times.csv. Each workstation has 1,440 minutes of total daily time. Daily maintenance consumes 10% of the available time at workstation 1, 14% at workstation 2, and 12% at workstation 3, so production may use only the remaining effective capacity of each workstation. Determine the nonnegative integer number of units of each radio model to produce per day so as to minimize total idle production time across the three workstations. For each workstation, idle time is its effective daily capacity minus the total processing time used at that workstation.

Thought: I have retrieved the per-unit processing times for each model at each workstation, as well as the maintenance percentages. I will define integer variables x_j for j = 1,...,101, where x_j is the number of units of HiFi-j to produce per day. The objective is to minimize the sum of idle times at the three workstations, where idle time at workstation i is its effective capacity minus the total processing time used at that workstation. The effective capacities are:
- Workstation 1: 1,440 × (1 - 0.10) = 1,296 minutes
- Workstation 2: 1,440 × (1 - 0.14) = 1,238.4 minutes
- Workstation 3: 1,440 × (1 - 0.12) = 1,267.2 minutes

The constraints ensure that the total processing time used at each workstation does not exceed its effective capacity. All x_j are nonnegative integers.

Final Answer:
Let x_j = number of units of HiFi-j to produce per day, for j = 1,...,101.

Parameters (from workstation_times.csv, preserving file and row order):

Let p_{i,j} = processing time (in minutes) required per unit of HiFi-j at workstation i, for i = 1,2,3 and j = 1,...,101, as given below:

- For Workstation 1 (i=1): p_{1,1} = 6, p_{1,2} = 4, ..., p_{1,101} = 9
- For Workstation 2 (i=2): p_{2,1} = 5, p_{2,2} = 5, ..., p_{2,101} = 3
- For Workstation 3 (i=3): p_{3,1} = 4, p_{3,2} = 6, ..., p_{3,101} = 6

Effective capacities:
- C_1 = 1,296
- C_2 = 1,238.4
- C_3 = 1,267.2

Variables:
- x_j ∈ {0, 1, 2, ...} for j = 1,...,101

Objective:
Minimize total idle time across all workstations:
\[
\text{Minimize} \quad \sum_{i=1}^3 \left[ C_i - \sum_{j=1}^{101} p_{i,j} x_j \right]
\]
which is equivalent to:
\[
\text{Minimize} \quad (C_1 + C_2 + C_3) - \sum_{i=1}^3 \sum_{j=1}^{101} p_{i,j} x_j
\]
or, equivalently, maximizing total processing time used (since the constant term is fixed):
\[
\text{Minimize} \quad 3,801.6 - \sum_{i=1}^3 \sum_{j=1}^{101} p_{i,j} x_j
\]

Subject to:
\[
\sum_{j=1}^{101} p_{1,j} x_j \leq 1,296
\]
\[
\sum_{j=1}^{101} p_{2,j} x_j \leq 1,238.4
\]
\[
\sum_{j=1}^{101} p_{3,j} x_j \leq 1,267.2
\]
\[
x_j \geq 0,\quad x_j \in \mathbb{Z} \quad \forall j = 1,\ldots,101
\]

Where the p_{i,j} coefficients are as follows (from the CSV, in original order):

Workstation 1 (row 1): [6, 4, 6, 7, 6, 6, 8, 9, 6, 7, 1, 2, 4, 7, 3, 8, 3, 2, 4, 5, 8, 3, 2, 3, 9, 7, 3, 5, 7, 6, 2, 1, 5, 6, 5, 1, 7, 9, 8, 3, 3, 8, 2, 3, 3, 8, 9, 2, 3, 4, 2, 9, 2, 1, 8, 8, 4, 4, 6, 1, 6, 5, 3, 5, 1, 6, 6, 5, 3, 4, 3, 8, 1, 2, 3, 2, 8, 4, 4, 2, 7, 5, 1, 6, 4, 1, 3, 8, 3, 3, 3, 3, 6, 7, 6, 2, 1, 8, 9, 7, 9]
